{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U10", "U11"],
      "pins": ["U10.4", "U10.5", "U10.8", "U11.4", "U11.5", "U11.8"],
      "nets": ["GND", "5V_switched_CAN"],
      "observed": "The isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 have their logic-side ground (GND1, pin 4) and isolated-side ground (GND2, pin 5) both connected to the same board GND net. Their isolated-side supply VCC2 (pin 8) is powered from 5V_switched_CAN, which is derived from the non-isolated +5V rail via Q9.",
      "expected": "For isolated CAN buses, the bus-side ground GND2 should be a separate isolated ground net (e.g., CAN_GND or GND_ISO) and VCC2 should be supplied by an isolated power source, not tied to board GND or non-isolated +5V.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"80\") (name \"GND\") ... (node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\")) ... (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9) through an isolated CAN transceiver (U11/companion)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The galvanic isolation of the CAN buses is bypassed, creating ground loops and defeating the intended isolation. This can cause communication errors, damage to transceivers or connected motor controllers, and violates the specified isolated CAN architecture.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R36"],
      "pins": ["R36.1", "R36.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTP", "VOUTN"],
      "observed": "R36 has a value field of `24k`, but its description and MPN (`FRC0805J240TSD`) specify a 24Ω resistor. The MPN decodes to 24Ω, while the circuit requires a 24kΩ bleeder across the high-voltage VOUTP/VOUTN rail.",
      "expected": "A 24kΩ resistor for the high-voltage bleeder between VOUTP and VOUTN. A 24Ω part would dissipate hundreds of watts and fail catastrophically.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"R36\") (value \"24k\") ... (description \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\") ... (field (name \"MPN\") \"FRC0805J240TSD\"))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If assembled according to the MPN, R36 will be a 24Ω resistor, causing excessive current, overheating, and likely failure. If the value field is used, the BOM conflict remains a sourcing/assembly anomaly.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design contains two anomalies: the isolated CAN transceivers have their isolated grounds tied to board GND (defeating isolation), and R36's value/MPN conflict (24k vs 24Ω) would cause a sourcing/assembly error."
}

